🔑 Why we use HMAC
Integrity (Data not changed)
It ensures that the message was not modified during transmission.
Even a small change in data → completely different HMAC.
Authentication (Who sent it)
Uses a secret key shared between sender and receiver.
If HMAC matches → message came from someone who knows the secret key.
Protection against tampering
Attackers cannot modify data without knowing the secret key.
⚙️ How it works (simple)

Sender:

HMAC = hash(secret_key + message)
Receiver:
Recalculates HMAC using same key
Compares with received HMAC

If both match ✅ → message is valid
If not ❌ → message is tampered

🔐 Real-world use cases
APIs authentication (like request signing)
JWT signatures
Webhooks verification (Stripe, GitHub)
Secure communication between services
💡 Why not just hash?
A simple hash (like SHA-256) is not secure for authentication
Anyone can recompute hash if they know the message
HMAC adds a secret key, making it secure
🧠 Interview one-liner

HMAC is used to verify both the integrity and authenticity of a message using a shared secret key.

